@@ -0,0 +1,168 @@
+# Stock reservations
+
+Status: resolved
+
+## Problem Statement
+
+Checkout has to promise stock to a cart *before* payment goes through, but the inventory package can only promise stock through a placed Order. If checkout places the Order before payment, a failed or abandoned payment leaves stock allocated until someone remembers to cancel. If it places the Order after payment, the stock may already be gone by the time payment succeeds. Checkout needs a short-lived claim on stock that:
+
+- turns into an Order once payment succeeds,
+- can be given up explicitly,
+- disappears by itself if nobody acts on it.
+
+The cart service retries calls over an unreliable network, so a retried request must never create a second claim.
+
+## Solution
+
+Add **Reservations** to the inventory package. A Reservation is a time-limited claim on stock for one cart in one warehouse.
+
+- **Reserving** holds as many units of each requested SKU as are Available right now, up to the Requested quantity. A Reservation can therefore hold less than the cart asked for, and the cart works out the shortfall itself.
+- Units held by active Reservations are **Reserved**. They are no longer Available to other Reservations or to directly placed Orders.
+- **Confirming** turns an active Reservation into a placed Order for exactly the units it holds.
+- **Releasing** gives an active Reservation up and frees its units immediately.
+- A Reservation that is neither confirmed nor released **expires** 20 minutes after it was created. From that moment it stops holding stock. Nothing has to run for that to happen: every query and command looks at the injected clock.
+- Every reserve request carries an **idempotency key**. Repeating a request with the same key returns the original Reservation. Reusing a key for a different request is rejected.
+
+## User Stories
+
+1. As the checkout flow, I want to reserve stock for a cart before taking payment, so that the goods are still there when payment succeeds.
+2. As the checkout flow, I want one reserve call to cover several SKUs from one warehouse, so that one cart is one Reservation.
+3. As the checkout flow, I want each SKU in my request to hold as many units as are Available, up to what I asked for, so that a cart can proceed with what is in stock.
+4. As the checkout flow, I want SKUs that have nothing Available to be left out of the Reservation's items entirely, so that the items show only what is actually held.
+5. As the checkout flow, I want to work out the shortfall by comparing the Reservation's items with what I requested, so that I can tell the customer what is missing.
+6. As the checkout flow, I want an InsufficientStock error when none of my lines can hold anything, so that I don't get an empty Reservation.
+7. As the checkout flow, I want a request with an unknown warehouse, an unknown SKU, or a quantity that isn't a positive integer to be rejected entirely, so that a malformed cart never holds anything.
+8. As the checkout flow, I want an empty request to be rejected with InvalidOrder, like an empty order, so that errors stay consistent.
+9. As the checkout flow, I want each Reservation to have a stable id and an expiry time, so that I can refer to it later and show the customer a countdown.
+10. As the checkout flow, I want a Reservation to expire 20 minutes after it was created, so that abandoned carts don't hold stock forever.
+11. As the checkout flow, I want to confirm an active Reservation into a placed Order, so that paid carts become real orders.
+12. As the checkout flow, I want the confirmed Order to contain exactly the units the Reservation held, so that the order matches what was promised.
+13. As the checkout flow, I want confirming never to fail for lack of stock, so that a customer who has paid is never refused the goods they were promised.
+14. As the checkout flow, I want confirming an already-confirmed Reservation to return the same Order, so that a retried confirm never creates a second order.
+15. As the checkout flow, I want confirming an expired Reservation to raise ReservationExpired, so that I know to reserve again instead of silently getting an order.
+16. As the checkout flow, I want confirming a released Reservation to raise InvalidReservationState, so that a cart I gave up can't come back to life.
+17. As the checkout flow, I want to release an active Reservation and have its units become Available immediately, so that other customers can buy them.
+18. As the checkout flow, I want releasing an already-released Reservation to return it unchanged, so that a retried release is harmless.
+19. As the checkout flow, I want releasing an expired Reservation to return it unchanged and still EXPIRED, so that I don't need special handling when the Reservation has already timed out.
+20. As the checkout flow, I want releasing a confirmed Reservation to raise InvalidReservationState, so that I don't accidentally free stock that belongs to an Order (I cancel the Order instead).
+21. As the checkout flow, I want to look up a Reservation by id and see its current status, including EXPIRED once its time has passed, so that I can show the customer the right state.
+22. As the checkout flow, I want ReservationNotFound for an unknown reservation id on get, confirm, or release, so that typos and stale ids are obvious.
+23. As the cart service, I want repeating a reserve request with the same idempotency key to return the original Reservation, so that network retries are safe.
+24. As the cart service, I want that replay to return the Reservation as it is now (confirmed, released, or expired), without holding more stock or extending expiry, so that a key always means one checkout attempt.
+25. As the cart service, I want a replay to be recognised by the request I sent (warehouse and requested items), not by what was actually held, so that retrying after a partial hold still matches.
+26. As the cart service, I want reusing a key with a different request to raise IdempotencyConflict, so that a bug that reuses keys can't silently return someone else's Reservation.
+27. As the cart service, I want a reserve that failed (validation or InsufficientStock) not to use up its key, so that I can retry the same key once stock arrives.
+28. As a back-office user, I want Available to subtract Reserved units, so that nobody promises stock that a cart is holding.
+29. As a back-office user, I want directly placed Orders to be unable to take Reserved units, so that checkout's promises are honoured.
+30. As a back-office user, I want expired Reservations to stop counting as Reserved the moment they expire, with no clean-up job, so that stock figures are always current.
+31. As a back-office user, I want the stock report to show a Reserved column, so that on-hand, allocated, reserved, and available add up on every row.
+32. As a back-office user, I want a confirmed Reservation's units to show up as Allocated (through its Order) and no longer as Reserved, so that nothing is counted twice.
+33. As a back-office user, I want shipping or cancelling an Order created from a Reservation to work like any other Order and leave the Reservation CONFIRMED, so that the order lifecycle doesn't change.
+34. As a developer, I want all reservation timing to come from the injected clock, so that tests can move time deterministically.
+35. As a developer, I want existing callers of `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` to keep working unchanged, so that adding reservations doesn't break anything.
+
+## Implementation Decisions
+
+**Domain records**
+- A new `ReservationStatus` enum with `ACTIVE`, `CONFIRMED`, `RELEASED`, `EXPIRED`, with lowercase string values like `OrderStatus`.
+- A new `Reservation` dataclass with exactly `reservation_id`, `warehouse`, `items` (units held per SKU), `status`, `expires_at`, and `order_id` (None until confirmed). It is mutable, like `Order`. The requested quantities and the idempotency key are not stored on it; they live in the repository (see below).
+- Reservation ids are sequential, `RES-0001`, `RES-0002`, …, issued by the repository the same way order ids are.
+- Export `Reservation` and `ReservationStatus` from the package root.
+
+**Errors.** All four new errors subclass `InventoryError` and carry attributes the same way the existing errors do:
+- `ReservationNotFound`: carries the reservation id.
+- `ReservationExpired`: carries the reservation id and `expires_at`.
+- `InvalidReservationState`: carries the reservation id, the status, and the action attempted, mirroring `InvalidOrderState`.
+- `IdempotencyConflict`: carries the key.
+- Existing errors are reused: `UnknownWarehouse`, `UnknownSku`, `InvalidQuantity`, `InvalidOrder` (empty request), `InsufficientStock` (nothing could be held).
+
+**Repository.** It gains storage for Reservations:
+- save, get (raises `ReservationNotFound`), and list filtered by warehouse and status, plus an id sequence.
+- An idempotency index that maps each key to its Reservation id and to the original request (warehouse plus requested items).
+
+**Service construction.** `InventoryService(repository, ledger, clock)` is unchanged. A new optional keyword-only `reservation_ttl` defaults to 20 minutes.
+
+**Quantities**
+- A new query, `reserved(sku, warehouse)`, sums the units held by Reservations that are live right now.
+- `allocated` keeps its meaning: placed Orders only.
+- `available` = on-hand − allocated − reserved, floored at 0.
+- `place_order` keeps its all-or-nothing behaviour and already checks against `available`, so it automatically respects Reservations.
+
+**Expiry (lazy, no sweeper)**
+- `expires_at` = creation time + TTL.
+- A Reservation is live only while it is ACTIVE and `now < expires_at`. At exactly `expires_at` it has expired.
+- Every path that reads a Reservation sees an ACTIVE-but-past-due Reservation as EXPIRED: get, confirm, release, idempotent replay, and the `reserved` sum. Any path may persist the status change, but correctness must not depend on any particular call having happened first.
+- Expiry applies only to ACTIVE Reservations. CONFIRMED and RELEASED are final states.
+
+**reserve(warehouse, items, idempotency_key)**, in this order:
+1. Validate strictly, as `place_order` does. Unknown warehouse, unknown SKU, a non-positive or non-integer quantity, or an empty request rejects the whole call. Nothing is held and the key is not recorded.
+2. Idempotency lookup. If the key is known and the request matches (same warehouse, and requested items equal as a mapping regardless of order), return the stored Reservation in its current state, with no stock check, no top-up, and no change to expiry. If the key is known and the request differs, raise `IdempotencyConflict`.
+3. For each line, hold min(requested, Available now). Leave out lines that would hold 0.
+4. If no line holds anything, raise `InsufficientStock` naming the first line in request order, with that line's requested quantity and available = 0. Nothing is created and the key is not recorded.
+5. Otherwise create an ACTIVE Reservation and record the key with the original request.
+
+**confirm_reservation(id)**
+- ACTIVE and live: create a PLACED Order for exactly the held items, with the next order id and `placed_at` = now. Mark the Reservation CONFIRMED with that `order_id`. Don't re-check stock: the units move from Reserved to Allocated in one step, and any shortfall from write-offs surfaces at shipping, as it does for Orders today.
+- CONFIRMED: return the existing Order, even after `expires_at`.
+- EXPIRED (including ACTIVE past due): raise `ReservationExpired` and change nothing else.
+- RELEASED: raise `InvalidReservationState`.
+
+**release_reservation(id)**
+- ACTIVE and live: becomes RELEASED.
+- RELEASED or EXPIRED: return the Reservation unchanged.
+- CONFIRMED: raise `InvalidReservationState`.
+
+**get_reservation(id)**: returns the Reservation with its current status, or raises `ReservationNotFound`.
+
+**Stock report.** `StockRow` gains a `reserved` field as its **last** field, defaulting to 0, so existing rows built positionally (`StockRow(sku, on_hand, allocated, available)`) still compare equal when nothing is reserved.
+
+**Docs.** Update the README's Concepts section to cover Reserved and the Reservation lifecycle, using the GLOSSARY.md terms.
+
+## Testing Decisions
+
+- **One seam: the public `InventoryService` API** (plus `stock_report`), driven by the existing pytest fixtures (`repo`, `ledger`, `clock` as a `FixedClock`, `service`). Time is moved only with `clock.advance(...)`. No new seam is needed. Tests should not reach into the repository's idempotency index or into private service helpers.
+- A good test describes externally visible behaviour: return values, raised error types, and what `available`, `reserved`, `allocated`, `on_hand`, and the stock report show afterwards. Tests should not care whether expiry was persisted or computed on the fly.
+- Prior art: `tests/test_orders.py` (lifecycle, error types, clock-stamped fields), `tests/test_stock.py` (validation errors), `tests/test_reports.py` (`StockRow` equality). Add a new `tests/test_reservations.py` following the same style: a small `stocked` fixture and one behaviour per test.
+- Cases to cover:
+  - Full hold.
+  - Partial hold.
+  - Zero-available line dropped from items.
+  - All lines empty → InsufficientStock and nothing created.
+  - Strict validation holds nothing.
+  - Reservations reduce `available` for other Reservations and for `place_order`.
+  - Expiry exactly at 20 minutes, not before.
+  - `available` recovers on expiry with no other call made.
+  - get shows EXPIRED.
+  - Confirm creates an Order for the held units, and the units move from Reserved to Allocated.
+  - Confirm is idempotent.
+  - Confirm after expiry → ReservationExpired.
+  - Confirm after release → InvalidReservationState.
+  - Release in every state.
+  - Unknown id → ReservationNotFound.
+  - Idempotent replay returns the same Reservation, including after a partial hold and after stock arrives.
+  - Replay after expiry or confirm returns the current state.
+  - Key reuse with a different warehouse or items → IdempotencyConflict.
+  - A failed reserve doesn't use up its key.
+  - Custom `reservation_ttl`.
+  - The report's `reserved` column.
+- The whole existing suite must keep passing unchanged.
+
+## Out of Scope
+
+- Changing `place_order`: it stays all-or-nothing.
+- Extending, topping up, or editing an existing Reservation.
+- Reservations that span several warehouses.
+- A background sweeper or scheduled expiry job.
+- Expiring idempotency keys: they are remembered for the lifetime of the repository.
+- Any link back from an Order to the Reservation it came from, or cancelling a Reservation by cancelling its Order.
+- Ledger entries for Reservations. Reserving never moves physical stock.
+- Persistence beyond the in-memory repository.
+
+## Further Notes
+
+- Terms follow GLOSSARY.md: Reservation, Reserved, Requested quantity, Confirm, Release, Expire. Avoid "hold" as a noun and "lapse".
+- Confirmed by the domain owner:
+  - InsufficientStock names the first line in request order.
+  - Validation runs before the idempotency lookup.
+  - Replay never tops up a Reservation or extends its expiry.
+- Decisions recorded in `docs/adr/0001-reservations-expire-lazily.md` and `docs/adr/0002-reservations-hold-partially-orders-do-not.md`.
diff --git a/GLOSSARY.md b/GLOSSARY.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b135fb6
